+      <article id="journal-article" class="journal-article">
+        <p>Every cup at The Daily Grind begins thousands of miles away on high-altitude, shade-grown farms across Central America, East Africa, and Colombia. Rather than buying anonymous commodity lots through middlemen, our roasting team travels directly to origin each harvest season to walk the terraces alongside smallholder farmers and independent cooperatives. This direct-trade model guarantees that growers receive at least 40% above Fair Trade minimum prices, funding regenerative soil practices, heirloom varietal preservation, and local water-recycling wet mills.</p>
+        <p>Selecting a lot requires rigorous sensory and physical evaluation before a single sack is shipped. At the farm's cupping table, we score samples for clarity, sweetness, and vibrant organic acidity while checking green bean moisture levels and water activity to ensure stability during ocean transit. Once the parchment coffee is hulled and sorted by density and screen size, it is vacuum-sealed in grain-pro bags and loaded into temperature-monitored containers so the delicate floral and stone-fruit aromatics remain locked inside until arrival at our roastery.</p>
+        <p>When the green coffee reaches our roastery floor, we roast tiny sample batches to map out each bean's unique thermal transfer curve before committing to production runs. By tailoring airflow and drum speed to the specific density and processing method—whether washed, honey-processed, or sun-dried natural—we coax out caramelized sugars without masking the terroir of the farm. Every bag we put on the shelf is stamped with the harvest date, elevation, and producer's name so you can trace your morning brew right back to the hillside where it grew.</p>
+      </article>
